over time. We all know from our everyday experience that all other things being equal, we tend to remember experiences and information that are recent in time better than those that occurred long ago. Memories tend to fade over time. The second sin of omission I call absent-mindedness. So this is not memory fading over time. This is kind of a breakdown at the interface of memory and attention. So we may be distracted, focused on.
